From 47b49941642c5d67682dc0aa49abce5f099a6520 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Homies Dev <dev@homies.local>
Date: Wed, 26 Aug 2026 06:50:15 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 5/5] feat: add `npm run doctor`, a read-only environment and
 data diagnostic
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Debugging this project currently means clicking through the UI and
inferring backwards, which is slow and gives ambiguous answers. The
worst example is the Discover feed: an empty feed caused by an
over-eager dealbreaker filter looks exactly like an empty feed caused by
an empty database. Those are completely different problems and the
browser cannot tell you which one you have.

`npm run doctor` answers, without a browser:

  - Is Node the right major version, and does it match .node-version?
  - Is .env present, and which required keys are missing?
  - Which backend will the dev server actually talk to?
  - Is a local API server listening?
  - Does the database connect, and what is in it?
  - Per student, how many cards will their Discover feed contain — and
    when it is zero, whether that is because a dealbreaker removed
    everyone or because they have already swiped everyone.
  - Which mutual matches exist, and whether both halves chose the same
    preferred hostel (if they did not, no admin will ever see the pair,
    which is a confusing dead end to hit during a demo).

The proxy check earns its place. client/package.json proxies to the
Render deployment, so `node server.js` locally has no effect — the
browser never reaches it. Anyone testing "locally" is in fact exercising
the deployed backend against the deployed database, which is both slow
(free-tier cold start) and misleading (local code changes appear to do
nothing). That is not obvious from any symptom it produces.

Read-only by construction. It opens a mongoose connection, reads, and
disconnects. It never writes, so it is safe to run against the
production database.

No new dependencies — it uses mongoose and dotenv, which are already
present, plus node builtins. Adding a CLI framework for one script would
have been a worse trade.

Exits non-zero when it finds a problem, so it can be used as a
pre-deploy gate later if that turns out to be useful.

Also adds .env.example, which the README already implied existed. The
note that Gmail needs an App Password rather than an account password is
there because that is the failure everyone hits once.

Does not fix: it reports on the deployed database when MONGO_URI points
there, but it cannot tell you whether the deployed *code* is current.
